  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A typical summer-like pattern continues today, with \nhighs in the 90s and isolated showers and thunderstorms possible.\n\nA Bermuda high located to our east continues to dominate the weather \npattern today, bringing warm, moist air into the region from the \nsouth. Aloft, there will be quasi-zonal flow, with a few mid-level \ndisturbances expected to move over central NC through the day. This \nwill allow for the weather today to be similar to that of previous \ndays. High temperatures are expected to be in the low/mid 90s, with \nmaximum heat indices in the mid 90s to around 100. \n\nAdditionally, isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will \nbe possible this afternoon into the evening, with the best chance \ncloser to the weak Piedmont trough in the western Piedmont. Limited \nshear values (< 20 kts) will limit severe potential, however there \nwill be plenty of instability, with MUCAPE values expected to reach \naround 2000-2500 J/kg this afternoon. Thus, a few strong to severe \nwind gusts from water-loaded downdrafts cannot be ruled out. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Summertime heat and humidity, and diurnally-\nmaximized storms with strong to severe downbursts, are expected to \ncontinue for most of the coming week. \n\nAround a sub-tropical ridge that will retrograde from the swrn N. \nAtlantic to the Gulf Coast and lwr MS Valley, cntl NC will be \ninfluenced by a series of (mostly convectively-enhanced) mid-level \ndisturbances this week. Those disturbances will initially be within \nand related to wly flow along a positively-tilted trough/shear axis \nfrom the srn Plains and mid-South to the srn Middle Atlantic, then \namid wnwly flow from the upr MS Valley to the srn Middle Atlantic \nmid to late week. \n\nAt the surface, an Appalachian lee trough will extend across the \nCarolinas through most of the upcoming week, between high pressure \nacross the sub-tropical Atlantic and Gulf and a convectively-\nmodulated frontal zone that will waver from the central Plains to \nthe southern middle Atlantic. The models suggest that frontal zone \nmay edge into the Carolinas by the end of the week or next weekend.\n\nThe pattern described above will favor mostly terrain and lee trough-\nforced convection amid the convectively-perturbed wly flow through \nMon-Tue. While similar forcing mechanisms will also be influential \nmid to late week, influence from MCVs from more-vigorous upstream \nconvection/MCSs within the wnwly flow regime will probably maximize \nconvective and severe chances in cntl NC Wed-Wed night. \n\nIt will otherwise be hot for most of the week, with temperatures \nwell into the 90s and with heat index values in the upr 90s to near \n100 over the nw Piedmont to around 105 elsewhere, through at least \nThu. The approach and possible passage of the aforementioned frontal \nzone will probably yield less hot temperatures by Fri-Sat.",
